          "question": "Burning when I pee and going more often since yesterday, no temperature.",
          "answer": "Your symptoms, burning when you pee and needing the toilet more often, are described in the urinary tract infection reference as features of a lower urinary tract infection (Urinary tract infection, General clinical reference). I can't tell you whether that is what you have. Only a clinician can assess that.\n\nThe same reference says fever, chills, pain in the back or side (flank pain), nausea or vomiting, or feeling generally unwell point to a possible infection higher up, which needs prompt assessment. Having no fever does not rule this out. The sepsis guidance notes that a normal temperature should not be reassuring on its own, and that older adults may have no fever at all (Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection). If you develop any of those signs, or you feel rapidly worse, or you are confused or very drowsy, seek urgent medical care now.\n\nThe urgency check found no emergency pattern in your description. That does not mean nothing is wrong. Since your symptoms began only yesterday, it is reasonable to contact a doctor, pharmacist, or local urgent care service soon, especially if the symptoms persist or get worse. They can advise on testing and treatment. The sources I have do not cover treatment options, so I can't say what would be appropriate for you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
